-- Q1309: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct (ET 1.210), a financial interest in an audit client creates a self-interest threat because the auditor could be influenced to protect their own financial position rather than exercise independent judgment. Inheriting stock directly creates this threat regardless of how the interest was acquired.
Wrong (A): An advocacy threat arises when the auditor promotes or defends the client''s position, not from holding a financial interest.
Wrong (B): A familiarity threat arises from close personal relationships with client personnel, not from financial interests.
Wrong (D): An undue influence threat arises from external pressure or intimidation by the client, not from the auditor''s own financial holdings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1309;

-- Q1311: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct (ET 1.210), a self-review threat exists when a CPA firm audits financial statements it also prepared, because the auditor may not objectively evaluate their own work product. For non-issuers, this service is permitted if management takes responsibility and the firm applies safeguards.
Wrong (B): An undue influence threat arises from external pressure or intimidation, not from auditing one''s own work.
Wrong (C): A familiarity threat arises from close personal relationships developed over time, not from reviewing previously prepared work.
Wrong (D): An advocacy threat arises from promoting or defending a client''s position in a dispute, not from reviewing one''s own work product.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1311;

-- Q1312: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct (ET 1.210), when a client pressures the auditor to change the opinion by threatening to switch auditors, this constitutes an undue influence (intimidation) threat. The client is attempting to exert pressure that could cause the auditor to subordinate professional judgment.
Wrong (A): An advocacy threat arises when the auditor promotes the client''s position, not from client pressure on the auditor.
Wrong (C): A familiarity threat arises from close personal relationships developed over a long engagement, not from client intimidation.
Wrong (D): A self-interest threat involves the auditor''s own financial interests, not external pressure from the client.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1312;

-- Q1313: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct (ET 1.210), serving as an expert witness on behalf of an audit client creates an advocacy threat because the CPA is promoting or defending the client''s position rather than acting as an independent evaluator. This role inherently requires taking the client''s side.
Wrong (A): A self-review threat arises when the auditor evaluates their own work product, not from testifying on the client''s behalf.
Wrong (B): A familiarity threat arises from close personal relationships with client personnel, not from legal advocacy.
Wrong (D): A self-interest threat involves the auditor''s own financial interests, not promoting the client''s legal position.$EXPL$
